
*************************************************** Variables **********************************************************

Definition: A variable is a named memory location that stores a value.

Syntax:
dataType variableName = value;

Types of Variables:

Local Variable → Declared inside a method, constructor, or block.
Lifetime: only within that block.

void method() {
    int x = 10; // local
}


Instance Variable → Declared inside a class but outside methods. Belongs to object.
class Test {
    int count; // instance variable
}

Static Variable (Class Variable) → Declared with static, shared across all objects.

class Test {
    static int counter = 0;
}
*************************************************** Data Types **********************************************************

Java is statically typed → every variable’s type must be declared.

1. Primitive Data Types

8 types (stored by value, not objects).

Type	Size	    Range (Approx)	                        Default	Example
byte	1 byte	    -128 to 127	0	                        byte b = 100;
short	2 bytes	    -32,768 to 32,7670	                    short s = 1000;
int  	4 bytes	    -2³¹ to 2³¹-1	0	                    int n = 100000;
long	8 bytes	    -2⁶³ to 2⁶³-1	0L	                    long l = 100000L;
float	4 bytes	    ~6–7 decimal digits	0.0f    	        float f = 3.14f;
double	8 bytes	    ~15–16 decimal digits	0.0d	        double d = 3.14159;
char	2 bytes	    Unicode (0 to 65,535)	'\u0000'        char c = 'A';
boolean	1 bit	    true/false	false	                    boolean flag = true;

✅ Notes:

Use L for long, f for float, d optional for double.
char stores Unicode → 'A', '\u0041'.
boolean is NOT 0/1 → strictly true/false.

2. Non-Primitive (Reference) Data Types

Objects, arrays, strings, etc.
    Store references (memory addresses).

Examples:

    String str = "Hello";      // String object
    int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};     // Array object
    Integer num = 10;          // Wrapper class

Type Casting

Converting one type to another.

Implicit (Widening) → smaller → larger (safe).

int a = 10;
double b = a; // int -> double


Explicit (Narrowing) → larger → smaller (may lose data).

double x = 10.5;
int y = (int) x; // double -> int

Useful One-Liners
Declare multiple variables: int a=5, b=10;
Constants: final int MAX = 100;

Default values apply only to instance/static vars, not locals.


*************************************************** Control & Conditional Statements **********************************************************

1. Control Statements

Control statements change the normal execution flow of a program.

a) Decision-making statements

Used to execute code blocks based on conditions.

if statement

if (condition) {
    // code executes if condition is true
}


if-else statement

if (condition) {
    // code if true
} else {
    // code if false
}


if-else-if ladder

if (condition1) {
    // code
} else if (condition2) {
    // code
} else {
    // default code
}


switch statement (better for multiple conditions on a single variable)

switch (expression) {
    case value1:
        // code
        break;
    case value2:
        // code
        break;
    default:
        // code
}


*************************************************** Loops **********************************************************

Used to repeat a block of code.

for loop

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}


while loop

int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}


do-while loop (executes at least once)

int i = 0;
do {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
} while (i < 5);


Enhanced for loop (for-each)

int[] arr = {10, 20, 30};
for (int num : arr) {
    System.out.println(num);
}

*************************************************** Jump Statements **********************************************************

Control the flow of loops.

break – exits from the loop/switch.

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) break;
    System.out.println(i);
}


continue – skips current iteration, goes to next.

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) continue;
    System.out.println(i);
}


return – exits from a method and optionally returns a value.

public int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}


✅ Quick Revision Notes

if-else → Two-way decision.

switch → Multi-way decision.

for → Definite number of iterations.

while → Unknown number of iterations.

do-while → Executes at least once.

break → Exit loop/switch.

continue → Skip iteration.

return → Exit method.

*************************************************** Strings **********************************************************

A String is a sequence of characters, stored as an object of the String class (in java.lang package).

Strings are immutable → once created, they cannot be changed.

String Creation
String s1 = "Hello";                 // String literal (stored in String Pool)
String s2 = new String("Hello");     // Using new keyword (stored in heap)

Common String Methods
==========================

Method                Description                                    Example
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
length()              Returns length of string                       "Hello".length() → 5
charAt(int index)     Returns char at given index (0-based)          "Java".charAt(2) → 'v'
substring(beg,end)    Returns substring [beg, end)                   "abcdef".substring(1,4) → "bcd"
contains(str)         Checks if substring exists                     "Hello".contains("ell") → true
equals(str)           Compares content                               "abc".equals("abc") → true
equalsIgnoreCase(str) Compares ignoring case                         "Java".equalsIgnoreCase("java") → true
compareTo(str)        Lexicographic compare                          "abc".compareTo("abd") → -1
indexOf(ch)           First index of char/substring                  "banana".indexOf('a') → 1
lastIndexOf(ch)       Last index of char/substring                   "banana".lastIndexOf('a') → 5
startsWith(prefix)    Checks prefix                                  "hello".startsWith("he") → true
endsWith(suffix)      Checks suffix                                  "hello".endsWith("lo") → true
toUpperCase()         Converts to uppercase                          "java".toUpperCase() → "JAVA"
toLowerCase()         Converts to lowercase                          "JAVA".toLowerCase() → "java"
trim()                Removes leading/trailing spaces                "  hi  ".trim() → "hi"
replace(old,new)      Replaces characters                            "java".replace('a','o') → "jovo"
split(regex)          Splits string into array                       "a,b,c".split(",") → {"a","b","c"}
isEmpty()             Checks if string is empty (length()==0)        "".isEmpty() → true

=============================
 StringBuilder & StringBuffer
=============================
- Mutable alternatives to String (can be modified).
- StringBuilder → Faster, not thread-safe.
- StringBuffer → Slower, thread-safe.

Both are mutable alternatives to String.

Common Methods:
append(), insert(), delete(), reverse(), toString()

Example:
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Hello");
sb.append(" World");
System.out.println(sb);   // Output: Hello World

String Formatting
String name = "Amit";
int age = 33;
System.out.printf("Name: %s, Age: %d", name, age);
// Output: Name: Amit, Age: 33

*************************************************** Arrays **********************************************************
An array is a collection of elements of the same type stored in contiguous memory locations.

Fixed size (cannot grow/shrink dynamically).

Index starts from 0.

Can hold primitive data types or objects.

1. Declaring & Initializing Arrays
// Declaration
int[] arr;          // preferred
int arr2[];         // also valid

// Allocation
arr = new int[5];   // array of size 5, default values = 0

// Initialization
int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};  // directly assign values

2. Accessing Elements
System.out.println(nums[0]);   // prints 1
nums[2] = 10;                  // update element at index 2

3. Array Length
System.out.println(nums.length);   // prints number of elements

4. Traversing Arrays
// For loop
for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    System.out.print(nums[i] + " ");
}

// Enhanced for loop
for (int n : nums) {
    System.out.print(n + " ");
}

5. Multidimensional Arrays
int[][] matrix = {
    {1, 2, 3},
    {4, 5, 6},
    {7, 8, 9}
};

System.out.println(matrix[1][2]);  // 6

6. Useful Methods (Arrays Class)
import java.util.Arrays;

int[] arr = {5, 2, 8, 1, 3};

// Sorting
Arrays.sort(arr);

// Searching (binary search) - array must be sorted
int idx = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 3);

// Fill
Arrays.fill(arr, 9);   // fills array with 9

// Compare
boolean equal = Arrays.equals(arr1, arr2);

// Convert to String
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr)); // prints [1, 2, 3]

7. Common Array Interview Tricks

Reverse an array

for (int i = 0, j = arr.length - 1; i < j; i++, j--) {
    int temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[j];
    arr[j] = temp;
}


Find max/min

int max = arr[0];
for (int i : arr) {
    if (i > max) max = i;
}


Sum of elements

int sum = 0;
for (int i : arr) sum += i;


✅ Quick Recap (One-liners)

int[] arr = new int[5]; → Declares array of size 5

arr.length → gives size

Arrays.sort(arr); → sort

Arrays.toString(arr) → print array

Fixed size, homogeneous elements, zero-based index

Array Methods in Java
1. Arrays.toString(array)

Converts array to string representation.

int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr)); // [1, 2, 3]

2. Arrays.sort(array)

Sorts array in ascending order.

int[] arr = {3, 1, 2};
Arrays.sort(arr);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr)); // [1, 2, 3]

3. Arrays.binarySearch(array, key)

Searches for key in sorted array.

int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4};
int index = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 3);
System.out.println(index); // 2

4. Arrays.equals(arr1, arr2)

Compares if two arrays are equal.

int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = {1, 2, 3};
System.out.println(Arrays.equals(a, b)); // true

5. Arrays.fill(array, value)

Fills array with given value.

int[] arr = new int[5];
Arrays.fill(arr, 7);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr)); // [7, 7, 7, 7, 7]

6. Arrays.copyOf(array, newLength)

Copies array into new array with new length.

int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};
int[] copy = Arrays.copyOf(arr, 5);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(copy)); // [1, 2, 3, 0, 0]

7. Arrays.copyOfRange(array, start, end)

Copies range from array.

int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
int[] sub = Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, 1, 4);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(sub)); // [20, 30, 40]

8. Arrays.deepEquals(arr1, arr2)

Compares nested arrays (2D+).

int[][] a = {{1,2}, {3,4}};
int[][] b = {{1,2}, {3,4}};
System.out.println(Arrays.deepEquals(a, b)); // true

9. Arrays.deepToString(array)

Converts multidimensional array to string.

int[][] arr = {{1,2}, {3,4}};
System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(arr)); // [[1, 2], [3, 4]]

10. Arrays.parallelSort(array)

Sorts large arrays faster using parallelism.

int[] arr = {9, 7, 5, 3, 1};
Arrays.parallelSort(arr);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr)); // [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]

*************************************************** Array List and HashMaps **********************************************************

List (Interface)

Definition: Ordered collection of elements (allows duplicates).

Implements: ArrayList, LinkedList, Vector.

Syntax:

List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();


Key Points:

Index-based (like arrays, but dynamic size).

Allows duplicate values.

Maintains insertion order.

🔹 ArrayList (Class - implements List)

Definition: Resizable array implementation of List.

Syntax:

ArrayList<Integer> arr = new ArrayList<>();


Important Methods:

add(E e) → Add element

add(int index, E e) → Add at index

get(int index) → Get element

set(int index, E e) → Update element

remove(int index) → Remove at index

remove(Object o) → Remove by value

contains(Object o) → Check existence

size() → Number of elements

isEmpty() → Check if empty

clear() → Remove all elements

indexOf(Object o) → First occurrence index

lastIndexOf(Object o) → Last occurrence index

toArray() → Convert to array

Iteration:

for (int i : arr) { System.out.println(i); }
arr.forEach(System.out::println);


🔹 HashMap (Class - implements Map)

Definition: Stores key-value pairs, no duplicate keys allowed, values can repeat.

Syntax:

HashMap<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();


Important Methods:

put(K key, V value) → Add/replace value

putIfAbsent(K, V) → Add only if key not present

get(K key) → Get value for key

getOrDefault(K, defaultValue) → Get with default

containsKey(K key) → Check key

containsValue(V value) → Check value

remove(K key) → Remove entry

remove(K key, V value) → Remove if key-value matches

size() → Number of entries

isEmpty() → Check empty

clear() → Remove all

keySet() → All keys

values() → All values

entrySet() → All key-value pairs

forEach((k,v) -> ...) → Iterate easily

Example:

map.put(1, "Amit");
map.put(2, "Sangwan");
map.forEach((k,v) -> System.out.println(k + " -> " + v));

******************* Array List and HashMaps  Important Methods **********************************************************


add(E e) → Adds element at the end.

add(int index, E e) → Inserts element at a specific position.

get(int index) → Returns element at given index.

set(int index, E e) → Replaces element at given index.

remove(int index) → Removes element at index.

remove(Object o) → Removes first occurrence of given object.

size() → Returns number of elements.

isEmpty() → Checks if list is empty.

contains(Object o) → Checks if element exists.

indexOf(Object o) → Returns first index of object, -1 if not found.

lastIndexOf(Object o) → Returns last index of object.

clear() → Removes all elements.

subList(int from, int to) → Returns a portion of the list.

toArray() → Converts ArrayList to array.

sort(Comparator c) → Sorts list with custom comparator.

HashMap – Important Methods

put(K key, V value) → Inserts key-value pair (replaces value if key exists).

putIfAbsent(K key, V value) → Inserts only if key not present.

get(Object key) → Returns value for key, or null if not found.

getOrDefault(Object key, V defaultValue) → Returns value or default.

remove(Object key) → Removes entry for key.

remove(Object key, Object value) → Removes entry only if key maps to value.

containsKey(Object key) → Checks if key exists.

containsValue(Object value) → Checks if value exists.

keySet() → Returns all keys.

values() → Returns all values.

entrySet() → Returns set of key-value pairs.

replace(K key, V value) → Replaces value for given key.

replace(K key, V oldValue, V newValue) → Replaces only if oldValue matches.

size() → Returns number of key-value pairs.

isEmpty() → Checks if map is empty.

clear() → Removes all entries.

forEach(BiConsumer action) → Iterates over entries (Java 8+).

merge(K key, V value, BiFunction remappingFunction) → Combines values.

compute(K key, BiFunction) → Updates value for key.

computeIfAbsent(K key, Function) → Adds value if key missing.

computeIfPresent(K key, BiFunction) → Updates only if key exists.



